onally incorrect claim as a comparison. Name its specific HSIE error, then show the corrected evidence-based claim.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on sources, places and respectful community knowledge, rather than guessing from a single picture. Keep every claim linked to evidence.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the sources, map features or community examples that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a detail is uncertain, identify what source you would need next. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Changing the wording without repairing the evidence does not fix the error. Giving feedback such as \"good\" does not help the speaker improve. Correct the claim or use of sources, not just the neatness of the poster.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Use the same HSIE idea on a new place, person, source or context, not a copy of the worked example. Review accuracy of evidence. Supported records follow the actual sources and purpose of the task.","options":["Use the same HSIE idea on a new place, person, source or context, not a copy of the worked example. Review accuracy of evidence.","A claim unrelated to the source or place.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no evidence."],"question":"What should you demonstrate in this teaching stage?"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nRehearsing helps speakers use a clear voice, a steady pace, eye contact and a good timing. Feedback is most useful when it names something that worked and one thing to improve, such as two stars and a wish. Now choose a different example to show you can transfer the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nInquire into or describe one fresh example that uses the same skill and write a short evidence-based reason.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe new example should use the same HSIE idea as the model, but contain different evidence.","explain":"Use the same HSIE idea on a new place, person, source or context, not a copy of the worked example. Review accuracy of evidence.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on sources, places and respectful community knowledge, rather than guessing from a single picture. Keep every claim linked to evidence.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the sources, map features or community examples that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a detail is uncertain, identify what source you would need next. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to new evidence. Correct the claim or use of sources, not just the neatness of the poster.","title":"Transfer to a new inquiry"}],"title":"Year 3, Week 39, Lesson 1: Practising the presentation","unit_number":8,"unit_title":"Inquiry project","week":39,"week_in_unit":4,"word_challenges":null,"worked_example":"WORK THROUGH THIS\nExample rehearsal: a student reads their conclusion aloud to a partner in three minutes, speaking slowly and pausing at each slide. Partner feedback: Star: loud and clear voice. Star: good photo. Wish: look up from the notes more.\n\nWrite what makes this example accurate, naming the sources, places or community details used.","worked_visuals":[{"alt":"Two stars and a wish.","caption":"Be specific.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16